  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper-level trough and low, currently located over southern \nIdaho, is projected to track east into the central Plains this \nweekend into early next week. As it approaches, a weak ridge of high \npressure will retreat south, permitting a series of disturbances to \ntraverse the Middle Ohio Valley (MOV) and Central Appalachians, amid \notherwise zonal flow. Meanwhile, a front lifts into the area on \nSUnday and the lingers nearby through the first part of the work \nweek.\n\nChances of showers and thunderstorms are expected to increase this \nevening and persist into the work week, with most substantial \nactivity occurring in the vicinity of the front, and in conjunction \nwith the passing disturbances. Heavy downpours and training storms \nwill be capable of depositing a couple inches of rain in a fairly \nshort amount of time. Thus, the primary concern during this time \nwill be a heightened risk of flash flooding. A few strong to severe \nstorms could also be capable of producing locally strong wind gusts.\n\nFrom mid to late week, the pattern remains unsettled as the upper \ntrough progresses into the MOV and then slowly spirals towards the \neast coast. Additional precipitation over already saturated soils \nwill sustain at least low-end flooding concerns for the majority\nof the work week.",
